  "fields": {
    "title": "Page Metaphor",
    "textContent": {
      "nodeType": "document",
      "data": {},
      "content": [
        {
          "nodeType": "heading-3",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "Description",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "In order to get to know a new medium quickly and to be able to assess its possibilities better, the use of metaphors can be a helpful tool. Thus, comparing new media with familiar ones can offer advantages. For example, the metaphor of the page, which is deeply rooted in the Web, or other references to the book were useful in the 1990s to help web users get started with this new medium. However, such a juxtaposition also brings with it problematic issues, because the Web is in fact not just a digital book format, but a mixed medium that combines diverse media formats and thus enables entirely new experiences and interactions. However, these can quickly be forgotten by comparison with the printed book. This can be seen, for example, in what used to be the early years of the web, when many companies merely embedded their print materials as PDFs on their websites, thereby exploiting very few of the web's possibilities. To this day, the metaphor of the page is deeply embedded in the design of websites and, as a result, still influences the way many designers conceive web projects.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
              "data": {
                "target": {
                  "sys": {
                    "id": "1IrVfC2CA1yeZD0tMLTakV",
                    "type": "Link",
                    "linkType": "Entry"
                  }
                }
              },
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "[1]",
                  "marks": [
                    {
                      "type": "superscript"
                    }
                  ],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": " Similar to the page metaphor, there is usually no change in the design process according to the different media. It is therefore not surprising that currently most websites still reflect this design process, which originated in print, in their design.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "blockquote",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "paragraph",
              "data": {},
              "content": [
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "»The control which designers know in the print medium, and often desire in the web\nmedium, is simply a function of the limitation of the printed page. We\nshould embrace the fact that the web doesn’t have the same constraints,\nand design for this flexibility.« ",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                },
                {
                  "nodeType": "entry-hyperlink",
                  "data": {
                    "target": {
                      "sys": {
                        "id": "2akOJ7fzyDq0NGD8E9Megx",
                        "type": "Link",
                        "linkType": "Entry"
                      }
                    }
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "nodeType": "text",
                      "value": "[2]",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "superscript"
                        }
                      ],
                      "data": {}
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "nodeType": "text",
                  "value": "",
                  "marks": [],
                  "data": {}
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "nodeType": "paragraph",
          "data": {},
          "content": [
            {
              "nodeType": "text",
              "value": "What both mediums have in common, however, is that both have led to a media revolution. They have influenced the way knowledge is disseminated and handled, and in doing so, have spurred major cultural changes.",
              "marks": [],
              "data": {}
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
